{"availability":"available","full":"showjihyun/world-politicians","name":"world-politicians","description":"World politicians +,- ","category":"Other","language":"TypeScript","stars":2,"forks":0,"created":"2026-08-26T12:42:26Z","updated":"2026-10-08T19:42:06Z","license":"MIT","branch":"main","homepage":"https://world-politicians.vercel.app","topics":[],"demo":"https://world-politicians.vercel.app/","color":"#323057","ink":"#d6caff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-08T23:43:23.350Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-08T23:43:23.350Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-08T23:43:23.350Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-08T23:43:23.350Z","stars":2,"forks":0}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["An interactive graph of who in American politics is allied with whom, who is feuding with whom — and how those bonds flip over time.","101 figures. 266 curated relationships, plus 112 co-sponsorship edges measured directly from congressional bill records. A nightly pipeline that reads the political press, asks an LLM who just fell out with whom, and folds the answer into a rolling one-year archive. Fully bilingual (English / 한국어). No backend — the whole thing is static files."],"features":["POLARIS was first shared on Reddit in late August 2026. The replies asked two hard questions: could this show who actually influences federal decisions? and how far can you trust a graph built on biased, clickbait…","New data layers — measured, not curated","Money, lobbying and defection sit on the profile, not in the graph. Each was tested as a relationship signal (shared donors, PAC share, defection versus intra-party feuds), and none of them predicted who is allied or…","Trust and measurement","label set (see Honest caveats for the numbers and their limits). The largest error group is a neutral article read as a feud, not a reversed ally/feud call."],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/showjihyun/world-politicians/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"42e0766d722ca983e0785b3e91d3d004bf816d1